   140	
   141	## Gate results
   142	
   143	### Gate A — PASS
   144	
   145	- A1: `SPEC.target` loads and executes the literal translated body; the loop
   146	  theorem uses exact definitional aliases of that same body and continuation.
   147	- A2/A3: the bridge-free connection claims retain an arbitrary continuation.
   148	  The `isinstance` bridge was narrowed after a probe exposed the reference
   149	  dereference boundary; reference arguments now remain on the fixed path.
   150	- A4: evaluator twins have exhaustive, non-overlapping equations. The sole
   151	  opaque destructor is value-determined on every reachable observation domain.
   152	  No fresh result can select an unconstrained final outcome.
   153	- A5: the satisfiable empty-dictionary false-postcondition mutation failed and
   154	  displayed the actual false result.
   155	- Fixed and bridge-enabled ground executions were byte-identical.
   156	
   157	### Gate B — PASS
   158	
   159	The theorem covers arbitrary finite symbolic key sequences, not examples,
   160	fixed sizes, or bounded unrollings. The postcondition directly formalizes the
   161	prompt's nonempty/all-lower-or-all-upper contract, including rejection of
   162	non-string and uncased string keys. The no-reference premise removes values
   163	that cannot be well-formed keys in the supplied model's empty initial heap; it
   164	does not bound the material dictionary domain.
   165	
   166	The intended execution model for this benchmark is the supplied MPY semantics.
   167	Its string case predicates are ASCII-oriented. The Python implementation itself
   168	was additionally checked against CPython on Unicode examples, but universal
   169	equivalence between MPY's character model and CPython Unicode is not claimed.
   170	
   171	### Gate C — PASS
   172	
   173	All named artifacts and commands exist in the workspace. The bridge-free
   174	connection proofs, positive proofs, negative mutation, LLVM/Haskell execution
   175	differential, prompt examples, and independent CPython differential corpus all
   176	ran in the final replay. No mismatch was hidden or converted into an
   177	assumption.
   178	
   179	## Trust boundary
   180	
   181	Trusted components are the supplied read-only MPY semantics, K's parser and
   182	LLVM/Haskell backends, the K logical kernel, and the stated interpretation of
   183	MPY constructors as the benchmark's Python subset. `py2mpy.py` is trusted only
   184	for the source-to-constructor translation; `solution.mpy` was regenerated in
   185	the replay, and the target embeds the same constructor body.
   186	
   187	`SPEC.loop` is not an external trust assumption: it has its own positive proof
   188	command. The shared proof theory contains no externally trusted primitive and
   189	no unconstrained result-bearing oracle.
   190	
   191	## Empirical support and excluded behavior
   192	
   193	`test_solution.py` uses an independently written CPython contract oracle. It
   194	checked the five prompt examples and every key sequence of length zero through
   195	three over a 16-element boundary pool: lowercase, uppercase, cased-plus-digit,
   196	uncased, mixed-case, Unicode, integers, `True`, `None`, and a tuple. There were
   197	4,374 comparisons and zero mismatches. This is finite evidence, not the formal
   198	unbounded proof.
   199	
   200	Excluded from the formal claim are non-dictionary arguments, dangling or
   201	mutable heap-reference keys, and claims that the supplied ASCII case model is a
   202	complete model of CPython Unicode. These exclusions do not hide a finite-size
   203	bound on dictionaries or strings.
